import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Button
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Trace
import ../../Ui/Who
import ../../Ui/Arch
import ../../Ui/ArchBox

meta = {
	title: "The platform",
	description: "What a rocx app runs on: a server platform, Roc compiled to native code in a Rust host with Postgres or SQLite, values in memory, actors and plugins (jobs, email); and a browser platform, the same pages hydrated from WebAssembly.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "two", label: "Two platforms, one app" },
		{ id: "server", label: "What the server gives" },
		{ id: "app", label: "An app" },
		{ id: "request", label: "A request, all the way" },
		{ id: "build", label: "Building and running" },
		{ id: "why", label: "Why this shape" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform"
				title="Two platforms, one app: native on the server, wasm in the browser"
				actions={[
					<Button href={Paths.platform_server} primary>The server</Button>,
					<Button href={Paths.platform_browser}>The browser</Button>,
				]}
			>
				A Roc program runs on a platform: the host that gives it the effects it may have. A rocx app is
				two programs. The server's is Roc compiled to native code, linked with a Rust host into one
				executable, which answers HTTP and does what a single server does: a database (Postgres or SQLite),
				state that outlives a
				request, and what packages plug in (jobs, cron, email). The browser's is the same pages, compiled to WebAssembly with a small
				Zig host and a page of JavaScript, which take over what the server rendered. The two talk over HTTP.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="two">Two platforms, one app</Heading>
			<Arch
				left={[
					<ArchBox kind="roc" name={[<code>server/</code>, <>: native</>]}>
						<p>
							Roc compiled to machine code, in a Rust host (tokio, hyper). Pages rendered whole, endpoints;
							Postgres or SQLite, values in memory, live rooms and channels, actors, plugins (jobs and cron, email). One executable,
							one server.
						</p>
					</ArchBox>,
				]}
				wire="HTTP"
				right={[
					<ArchBox kind="wasm" name={[<code>web/</code>, <>: wasm</>]}>
						<p>
							The app's components, compiled to WebAssembly with a Zig host, and <code>web/runtime.js</code>,
							which hydrates the page the server sent, runs its holes again when signals change, and sends its
							requests.
						</p>
					</ArchBox>,
				]}
			/>
			<p>
				The same components compile on both: the modules they are written with (<code>Html</code>,
				<code>Solid</code>, <code>Show</code>, <code>For</code>, <code>Loading</code>, <code>Errored</code>,
				<code>Context</code>, <code>Transition</code>, <code>Router</code>) and HTTP's (<code>Server</code>,
				<code>Endpoint</code>, <code>Call</code>, <code>Middleware</code>, <code>Channel</code>) are the
				browser platform's, and the build copies them into the server platform, whose host renders them as
				the browser's runtime would, byte for byte.
			</p>

			<Heading id="server">What the server gives</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Requests and streams" href={Paths.platform_server}>
					<p>
						Every request a call of its own, which pauses while it waits; streams of events as loops that
						sleep; a crash that ends one call, not the server, and frees what it held.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="HTTP, both ways" href={Paths.platform_http}>
					<p>
						Requests to other servers, with limits; bodies as the bytes they were; answers fetched from elsewhere
						and passed on as they come; cookies with every attribute.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Settings and crypto" href={Paths.platform_secrets}>
					<p>
						The environment, a dev server's own paths, random bytes and AES-GCM, and the crypto package an
						app signs with: SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="The database" href={Paths.platform_database}>
					<p>
						Postgres, or SQLite in the server's process (or Turso's engine), as the program says: queries whose rows decode into
						the record you annotate, transactions, migrations built into the app; or roc-pg, whose queries are
						checked at build.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Plugins" href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>
					<p>
						What a package adds to an app in one line: routes, tables, background loops, pages for
						development. Jobs and cron are one (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>packages/jobs</a>).
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Live and actors" href={Paths.platform_live}>
					<p>
						Topics, rooms and channels (who is here, what was said, live data for pages), values in memory
						with a time to live (<code>Kv</code>), and actors: state one message at a time, kept in the database.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>
			<p>
				It is opinionated on purpose: one server, Roc, and one database, Postgres or SQLite. The host, in
				Rust, gives Roc general things: HTTP, database connections, sockets with TLS, background loops that sleep until woken, calls
				isolated from their caller, actors' mailboxes and timers, values in memory. Migrations, plugins and
				actors' state are the platform's Roc, written on those
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#layers"}>what is Rust, what is Roc</a>). What needs neither the
				host nor the compiler is a package, not the platform: files in S3, the crypto written in Roc, and
				jobs and cron and email (plugins) are <a href={Paths.ecosystem}>the ecosystem's</a>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="app">An app</Heading>
			<p>
				On the server platform an app is a record: how it answers a request, its migrations, its kinds of
				actor, and its plugins (the jobs plugin: the kinds of job its workers run, and its schedules). The
				platform's tests' app has all of them:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_program.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_program.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>This site has pages and endpoints, and none of the rest; <code>rocx build</code> writes its program:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_site_program.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_site_program.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>respond!</code> takes the request, a <code>Server.Request</code> (its method, path, query,
				headers, body, and <code>peer</code>, the address of the connection it came on, which the host hands
				over in one record with the rest), and what the server was started with
				(<code>--context-file</code>), and answers with a <code>Server.Response</code>: the routes rocx
				writes answer it (<a href={Paths.routing}>routing</a>). An answer that is a stream carries it: a function the host
				runs as a call of its own, with its <code>send!</code> and <code>sleep!</code>. <code>actors</code>
				lists the app's kinds of actor, which <code>rocx build</code> finds, so the host can run one when
				nobody waits on it (a tell, a timer). The browser's app is the page's components, the same root the
				server rendered:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.client_app.file} source={Sources.client_app.rocx} />

			<Heading id="request">A request, all the way</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> hyper hands the host a request; the host reads its body
					(16 MiB at most) and gives it to Roc, a call of its own on one of Roc's threads (it pauses
					whenever an effect waits), inside a guard that catches a crash.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The app's <code>respond!</code> runs its middleware and
					routes, and answers with the page: its layouts, the page, and the components in it, one tree. Its
					effects (a query, a job queued) pause that call, and no other.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The host renders the tree, every hole once: each endpoint a
					component reads is answered there and then, in-process, through its route, and the answers go in
					a script at the end of the page, with <code>/rocx/start.js</code>. A
					<code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> is rendered as plain HTML, for the server alone.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> The page shows at once. Its script fetches the wasm and
					hydrates the document: the same components run again, bound to the server's nodes, reading the
					answers from the page instead of asking. What is inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> is left as
					it came.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> A click writes a signal; what reads it runs again and
					patches its own node. A read whose answer isn't in the page (its URL built from a signal, or
					read again with <code>refresh!</code>) is a GET of the endpoint's own URL, which the server's app
					answers through the same route.
				</li>
			</Trace>

			<Heading id="build">Building and running</Heading>
			<Code
				title="In this repository"
				source={"just db        # a Postgres in Docker; prints its DATABASE_URL\njust dev       # this site on it: http://localhost:8080, rebuilt on save\nnode server/build.mjs app.roc out/app [--opt=speed] [--target=arm64musl]"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>server/build.mjs</code> builds the host with cargo into
				<code>server/platform/targets/</code>, then the app with <code>roc build</code>, which links the two
				into one executable. On macOS it links against the system; <code>--target=arm64musl</code> (or
				<code>x64musl</code>) makes a static Linux executable, cross-compiled, to copy into a container. The
				executable takes its port, its database and the rest as flags or environment
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="why">Why this shape</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Native on the server.</strong> Roc compiles to machine code, so the server runs it as such:
					each request a call of its own on a few threads of Roc's, pausing while an effect waits, and Rust
					where Roc can't reach (sockets, TLS, threads). No wasm runtime between them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Wasm in the browser.</strong> The browser runs WebAssembly: the components that hydrate, and
					nothing an endpoint's handler does. A <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> stays the server's.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One component model.</strong> Pages are Solid-style components on both, so the server
					renders what the browser will hydrate, with the answers it will need.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One server.</strong> An app runs as one instance, by design: live rooms, topics, channels,
					values in memory and actors' mailboxes are in its memory; jobs and actors' state are in its database.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="note" title="A working prototype">
				<p>
					Both platforms are tested, together and apart: the server's against Postgres, SQLite and Turso, crashes and all; the
					browser's in Chromium; this site on both. Each page here says what isn't there yet.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
